A stack can remove values as soon as a larger value appears to their right. When the current number is larger than the stack top, that top cannot be a leader anymore. Equal values are kept because both can satisfy the leader condition.

This processes the array from left to right and leaves the leaders in their original order. The input array is not modified.

```javascript
class Solution {
    leaders(nums) {
        const stack = [];

        for (const value of nums) {
            while (stack.length > 0 && stack[stack.length - 1] < value) {
                stack.pop();
            }
            stack.push(value);
        }

        return stack;
    }
}
```

For `[16, 17, 4, 3, 5, 2]`, adding `17` removes `16`, and adding `5` removes both `4` and `3`. The remaining stack is `[17, 5, 2]`.

An empty input returns an empty stack, and a single value is returned as the only leader.

Time complexity: `O(n)`, since each value is pushed and popped at most once. Space complexity: `O(k)` for the stack and returned leaders.

The value at index `i` is a leader when it is at least as large as the maximum value from `i` to the end. A suffix maximum array stores that maximum for every position, so each final check is simple.

The input array is only read and is not changed. This version uses an extra array, which makes the logic easy to follow.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> leaders(int[] nums) {
        List<Integer> answer = new ArrayList<>();
        int n = nums.length;

        if (n == 0) {
            return answer;
        }

        int[] suffixMaximum = new int[n];
        suffixMaximum[n - 1] = nums[n - 1];

        for (int i = n - 2; i >= 0; i--) {
            suffixMaximum[i] = Math.max(nums[i], suffixMaximum[i + 1]);
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (nums[i] >= suffixMaximum[i]) {
                answer.add(nums[i]);
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

The last element always passes because there is nothing after it. Repeated values also remain leaders when they are tied with the maximum on their right.

Time complexity: `O(n)`. Space complexity: `O(n)` for the suffix maximum array, in addition to the returned list.

A direct check is useful here. For every number, look through the values on its right. If none is greater, keep the number as a leader. Equal values are allowed, so the comparison only rejects a value when a strictly larger number appears.

```cpp
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> leaders(vector<int>& nums) {
        vector<int> answer;
        int n = nums.size();

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            bool isLeader = true;

            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                if (nums[j] > nums[i]) {
                    isLeader = false;
                    break;
                }
            }

            if (isLeader) {
                answer.push_back(nums[i]);
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

For `[16, 17, 4, 3, 5, 2]`, the checks keep `17`, `5`, and `2`.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` in the worst case. Space complexity: `O(k)` for the returned leaders, not counting the input array.

