## Why Linked List?
LinkedList is used because it allows O(1) insertion at the front using addFirst(), enabling us to maintain the correct leader order without reversing the list.


## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public List<Integer> leaders(int[] nums) {
        LinkedList<Integer> leadersList = new LinkedList<>();

        if (nums.length == 0) {
            return leadersList;
        }

        leadersList.add(nums[nums.length-1]);
        int maxRight = nums[nums.length-1];

        for(int j = nums.length - 2; j >= 0; j--){
            if (nums[j] > maxRight) {
                maxRight = nums[j];
                leadersList.addFirst(maxRight);
            }
        }

        return leadersList;
    }
}
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** 
O(n) due to the single loop iterating through the input array.

**Space Complexity:**
O(n) in the worst case, as the leadersList can store up to n elements if the input array is strictly increasing.

